<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/840">840</a>–<a href="/years/879">879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarid dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. A coppersmith turned leader of local volunteer fighters, he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaranj">Zaranj</a> in 861 and conquered much of Iran and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, overthrowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirids</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> in 873. His march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> in 876, and he died in 879.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar.jpg" alt="Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar" width="320" height="517" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sotheby's · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_illustrated_and_illuminated_folio_from_a_manuscript_of_Husayn_Va%E2%80%99iz_al-Kashifi%E2%80%99s_Akhlaq-i_Muhsini;_Ya%27qub_ibn_Layth_standing_on_the_roof_in_full_armour,_Persia,_Shiraz,_Safavid,_16th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ya'qub was born on 25 October 840, of eastern Iranian origin, in the small town of Karnin, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaranj">Zaranj</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Information about his genealogy is lacking, and sources describe him as extremely poor. His family moved to Zaranj because of sectarian violence between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>, and there he worked as a coppersmith, the trade that gave him the name Saffar. With his brothers he later joined the ayyars under Salih ibn al-Nadr, who opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. By 854 the ayyars had expelled the Tahirid governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, and in 861 Ya'qub overthrew another ayyar leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">Dirham</a> ibn Nasr, and took the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ya'qub first fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, and in 865 he defeated and killed their leader Ammar ibn Yaser. His armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and Bamiyan from the Hindu Shahis, and by 870 the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> was under his control. In 867 he sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mu'tazz a poem claiming the inheritance of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. In 873 he ousted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirids</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> and captured their ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tahir">Muhammad ibn Tahir</a>, which led to conflict with the Abbasid caliphate. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> in 875.</p><p>In 876 al-Muwaffaq offered him the governorship of Khurasan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a> and Ray; Ya'qub refused and advanced towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The armies met at Dayr al-Aqul on 8 April 876. His troops numbered about ten thousand, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> were superior in numbers. The Saffarid army, somewhat reluctant to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, broke and fled, and many men drowned in flooded land. Ya'qub withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ya'qub suffered from colic and refused treatment. He died at Gundeshapur on 5 June 879, and his brother Amr succeeded him. Accounts of his religion differ: some sources call him a Kharijite, a Christian or an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a>, while the historian D. G. Tor presents evidence that he was a devout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> warrior. During his rule Persian was introduced as an official language, and he reportedly did not know Arabic. He has the status of a popular folk hero because his court revived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-language">Persian language</a>, and poets traced his genealogy to the Iranian king Jamshid of tradition. His campaigns also marked an early stage in the decline of caliphal political unity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ya'qub ibn al-Layth</b>, a coppersmith (saffar) turned leader of the ayyar volunteer bands of Sistan, took control of Zaranj in 861 while fighting Kharijites.</blockquote><a href="/state/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Muhammad ibn Tahir the dynasty weakened, and in 873 <b>Ya'qub ibn al-Layth</b> of the Saffarid dynasty seized Nishapur.</blockquote><a href="/state/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 865 <b>Ya'qub ibn al-Layth</b>, founder of the Saffarid dynasty, killed the reigning Zunbil, and by about 870 he had conquered Zabulistan.</blockquote><a href="/state/zunbils">Zunbils</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Qarnin. Died in Gundeshapur, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.28,48.52&amp;year=865" aria-label="Gundeshapur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5797 990 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6247 1260h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6247 1260h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.28,48.52&amp;year=865">What was here in 865 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
